Okay, let's see. Charlie starts with 10 stickers. Then he buys 21 more. So I need to add those together. 10 plus 21 is 31. Then he gets 23 for his birthday. Adding that to the previous total: 31 plus 23. Let me do that math. 31 plus 20 is 51, then plus 3 more is 54. So now he has 54 stickers.

Next, he gives 9 to his sister. So subtract 9 from 54. 54 minus 9 is 45. Then he uses 28 to decorate a greeting card. Subtracting 28 from 45. Hmm, 45 minus 20 is 25, then minus 8 more is 17. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Let me check again. 45 minus 28. Maybe break it down: 45 - 20 = 25, then 25 - 8 = 17. Yeah, that's correct. So after all those actions, Charlie has 17 stickers left. Let me just make sure I didn't miss any steps. Started with 10, added 21 and 23, then subtracted 9 and 28. Yep, that adds up. So the final answer should be 17.

\boxed{17}

Okay, let's see. Nissa hired 60 seasonal workers as elves. First, a third of them quit because kids vomited on them. So, I need to calculate how many that is. A third of 60 is 60 divided by 3, which is 20. So, 20 elves quit. That leaves 60 minus 20, which is 40 elves remaining.

Then, 10 of the remaining elves quit after kids kicked their shins. So, subtract 10 from the 40. 40 minus 10 is 30. So, after both events, there should be 30 elves left. Let me double-check. Starting with 60, a third is 20, so 60-20=40. Then 10 more leave, so 40-10=30. Yep, that seems right.

